ong cả 5 ngữ cảnh và tách biệt với thao tác tại chỗ.","review_status":"COMPLETE","review_vote_sha256":"eb1f106d119df58ded1020c5271e8942ce0a0bcf495422badf222a11abb791cf","reviewer_slot":"reviewer_2","scope_decision":"ACCEPT","split_decision":"ACCEPT_SPLIT"},{"candidate_set_decision":"ACCEPT","context_evidence_decision":"ACCEPT","corrected_definition_en":"","corrected_part_of_speech":"","corrected_scope":"","definition_decision":"ACCEPT","part_of_speech_decision":"ACCEPT","review_input_sha256":"689501d471bd0569e50d8fdd7d7e106f7f8a3a20891f634975bd19455a98d61c","review_notes":"All 5 contexts (introduction index, linear regression scratch/concise, MLP b020, optimization adam b013) use 'in place' to mean already arranged/established/ready ('algorithm currently in place', 'all the parts/pieces in place', 'activation functions in place'), matching the proposed definition and scope SYSTEM_COMPONENT_OR_PROCESS_READINESS. This is clearly a different sense from the IN_PLACE_MUTATION sense sharing the same parent (memory/array mutation vs. state of readiness), so splitting the parent is supported -> ACCEPT_SPLIT. POS 'adverb' is an acceptable simplification for this idiomatic use. Candidates 'da duoc thiet lap', 'da san sang', and 'dang duoc ap dung' cover slightly different nuances (established / ready / currently in effect) but each is a defensible gloss attested by at least one context -> ACCEPT.","review_status":"COMPLETE","review_vote_sha256":"a18af5057eb536cd68504e1ce605135f571b641f503e0dbc2dbe17e37355519b","reviewer_slot":"reviewer_3","scope_decision":"ACCEPT","split_decision":"ACCEPT_SPLIT"}],"schema_id":"D2LTargetedRepairReviewConsensusV1","schema_version":"1.0.0","source_payload_sha256":"430f7edfed68f1612a9003965dd1421ba0c3eca7f6eaa982da0131bae890888f","source_term":"in place","split_label":"ESTABLISHED_CONFIGURATION"}
